Windows.media.playback.mediaplayer: MediaSource, AdaptiveMediaSource statics, a working MediaPlayer (Lightroom help videos)

The video in Lightroom Classic's Assisted Culling help overlay (Import dialog) did not play. Its
video toolkit opens every clip with Windows.Media.Core.MediaSource, which Wine did not have:
RoGetActivationFactory failed and Lightroom logged 0x80040154 from CreateMediaSource. Plain Media
Foundation decodes the clip fine; the codec was never the problem.

Adds, in windows.media.playback.mediaplayer.dll:
- Windows.Media.Core.MediaSource: CreateFromUri (the other constructors still fail), with
  IMediaSource2/3/4, IMediaPlaybackSource and IClosable.
- Windows.Media.Streaming.Adaptive.AdaptiveMediaSource statics. Lightroom tries an adaptive
  source first and calls CreateFromUriAsync without checking that the factory exists (with
  MediaSource alone it crashed there). IsContentTypeSupported answers yes only for HLS/DASH
  manifests; the Create*Async calls complete at once with UnsupportedManifestContentType, as
  Windows does for a plain file, and Lightroom falls back to MediaSource.CreateFromUri, then
  plays the clip through IMFMediaEngine.
- MediaPlaybackItem (with its video track list), MediaPlaybackSession, and a MediaPlayer that
  plays through IMFMediaEngine instead of stubs, including the frame server (VideoFrameAvailable,
  CopyFrameToVideoSurface). Engine callbacks only queue events; a worker thread per player
  delivers them and pumps frames, so app code never runs under the engine's lock.
- The interface definitions these need in windows.media.core.idl and windows.media.playback.idl,
  with vtable layouts taken from the Windows metadata; members whose types Wine does not declare
  yet are typed IInspectable.
- MediaPlayer no longer creates its transport controls (and a window) when it is constructed.

Verified: a test program that plays the help clip through Uri -> MediaSource -> MediaPlaybackItem
-> MediaPlayer frame server -> CopyFrameToVideoSurface reads back the clip's real colours,
scaled, in a 750x450 D3D11 surface (11.10-106: MediaSource missing). In Lightroom Classic the
Assisted Culling overlay video plays.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

--- a/dlls/windows.media.playback.mediaplayer/classes.idl
+++ b/dlls/windows.media.playback.mediaplayer/classes.idl
@@ -23,6 +23,15 @@
 
 import "windows.media.playback.idl";
 
+namespace Windows.Media.Core {
+    runtimeclass MediaSource;
+}
+
+namespace Windows.Media.Streaming.Adaptive {
+    runtimeclass AdaptiveMediaSource;
+}
+
 namespace Windows.Media.Playback {
     runtimeclass MediaPlayer;
+    runtimeclass MediaPlaybackItem;
 }
--- a/dlls/windows.media.playback.mediaplayer/engine.c
+++ b/dlls/windows.media.playback.mediaplayer/engine.c
@@ -0,0 +1,557 @@
